{"name":"Hot Fudge","hex":"#5e2912","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"hot fudge","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hot, thick, chocolate sauce that is usually served on ice cream or other desserts."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hot_fudge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hot Fudge","blocks":[{"text":"Hot Fudge is an American children's television series that was produced in Detroit by WXYZ-TV and distributed by the Lexington Broadcast Services Company. Originally airing only in Detroit beginning in 1974, the show grew in popularity and was syndicated nationally from 1976 to 1980; a few of WXYZ's sister ABC-owned stations carried the program."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hot_Fudge","wikidata":"Q5909971"},{"title":"Fudge","blocks":[{"text":"Fudge is a confection made by mixing sugar, butter, and milk. Modern fudge has its origins in the 19th century United States, and became popular in American women's colleges late in the century. Fudge can come in a variety of flavorings depending on the region or country it was made; popular flavors include fruit, nut, chocolate, and caramel. Fudge is often bought as a gift from a gift shop in tourist areas and attractions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jpg/330px-Handmade_fruit_fudge_squares.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fudge","wikidata":"Q379138"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png/500px-Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png/500px-Karo_Corn_Syrup_fudge_advertisement.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"College girls\" mak